  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSurface high pressure remains in place through Thursday resulting in \nquiet weather across the area. Lows in the 50s tonight with highs on \nThursday in the lower to mid 80s.\n\nElevated moisture moves in around the 700-800 mb layer late Thursday \nnight into Friday morning. This could result in spotty showers and \nisolated thunderstorms. Shear is weak and CAPE values running about \n500-1000 J/kg. Very dependent upon this small layer of moisture\nhowever, so confidence is low for now. \n\nSoutherly flow become established at the surface on Friday with \ngusts around 25 to 30 mph likely. With south to southeast flow at \nthe surface any instability that develops through the day will \nlikely remain capped, however with some potential for a wave to move \nthrough on Friday afternoon this capping could weaken and allow a \nfew thunderstorms to develop. With moderate shear and 1000-1500 \nJ/kg CAPE, an isolated severe storm will be possible. A secondary \nwave and slightly deeper instability on Friday night may lead to \nadditional shower and thunderstorm chances with additional severe \nweather threats.\n\nTemperatures warm across the area on Saturday as the wave moves by \nto the north. Even with stronger west southwest flow aloft and \ndecent mid level warm advection, weak mid to upper level ridging \nshould suppress any convective activity. Highs should climb into the \n90s near and west of I-90.\n\nSunday may be a day to keep an eye on we a wave tracks by along the \nCanadian border and a weaker sub tropical like wave drifts northeast \ninto the area. With another day expected to climb into the 90s and \nsome weak forcing aloft, we could see a few storms develop. For now \nconfidence is too low to include in the forecast.\n\nSouthwest flow aloft remains in place through the middle of next \nweek and the next wave of interest has some agreement and may arrive \nMonday night into Tuesday. Otherwise temperatures will remain above \nnormal Monday int Wednesday.",
